 ## Overview
-Voxel Face Culling with Chunked Rendering Order is a surface extraction technique that eliminates interior geometry by emitting mesh faces for solid voxels only when adjacent to an empty voxel or chunk boundary, while traversing the world in a camera-relative front-to-back order to optimize GPU depth sorting [S1, S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard blocky meshing by combining neighbor-based face culling with a spatial traversal order that prioritizes visible geometry, reducing overdraw and improving rendering performance compared to arbitrary chunk iteration [S1, S2, unofficial].
+This method generates mesh faces for solid voxels only when they are adjacent to an empty voxel or a chunk boundary, iterating through world chunks in a camera-relative front-to-back order to optimize GPU depth sorting and culling [S1, S2, unofficial]. It eliminates interior geometry by ensuring only visible faces reach the GPU, preventing non-visible geometry from being processed [S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard static meshing, it dynamically handles chunk boundaries by treating unallocated neighbor chunks as empty space, allowing for immediate culling of inner faces at chunk edges [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
